<Article slug="law-firm-intake-62-percent">
  <p>
    You have probably seen the claim that 62% of callers who reach a firm's voicemail hang up and call the next firm.
    We quoted it ourselves. When we traced it, it turned out to come from an answering-service company, and to describe
    callers to businesses in general, not law firms. The better-documented numbers are, if anything, worse.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a third-party researcher secret-shopped 500 US law firms for Clio's 2024 Legal Trends Report, only 40% answered
    the phone, down from 56% in 2019. Counting callbacks, 52% were reachable at all. Separately, a 2025 national industry
    study put unanswered law firm calls at 35%, and a CallRail survey of 1,000 consumers found only 42% leave a voicemail
    when a business does not answer, while 82% say they would call a competitor instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interesting part is not the headline number. It is that intake leaks in four distinct places, and most firms
    only think about one of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Leak 1: The unanswered call</h2>
  <p>
    The obvious one. Somewhere between a third and a half of calls go unanswered, depending on whose study you read,
    and the after-hours picture is worse. The compounding factor is that legal inquiries are often urgent and emotional:
    someone who was just arrested, served, or injured is not in a patient frame of mind.
  </p>
  <p>
    Responsiveness is also a hiring criterion in its own right. In Martindale-Avvo's 2024 legal consumer research, 58%
    of consumers named responsiveness as important to their hiring decision, alongside pricing and fees (61.6%) and
    reputation (53%).
  </p>

  <h2>Leak 2: The slow callback</h2>
  <p>
    Answering eventually is not the same as answering. The same Martindale-Avvo research found 85% of consumers expect a
    response within 48 hours and 40% will wait less than 24. The lead you return on Monday morning after a Friday
    evening voicemail has often already spoken to someone else.
  </p>

  <h2>Leak 3: The email nobody answered</h2>
  <p>
    This is the one firms consistently underestimate. In Clio's secret-shopper study only 33% of firms responded to an
    email inquiry at all, down from 40% in 2019. A separate analysis by the legal marketing firm Hennessey Digital found
    26% of firms never respond to an online lead. A quarter of firms are paying for a website with a contact form that
    functions as a black hole.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are running any paid acquisition, this is where the waste is. You are buying clicks to a form nobody reads.
  </p>

  <h2>Leak 4: The intake that started and stopped</h2>
  <p>
    The prospective client called, someone took details, a consult was mentioned, and then nothing. The engagement
    letter went out and was never signed. The documents were requested and never chased. Every firm has a pile of these
    and no system for them, because they are nobody's specific job.
  </p>

  <h2>What the fix is worth</h2>
  <p>
    Clio has found that firms which improve their client onboarding, including adding online intake tools, average 50%
    more incoming potential clients and 50% more revenue. Clio sells practice-management software, so read it with
    appropriate care, but the methodology is stated and the direction is consistent across sources.
  </p>
  <p>Modelled on a three-attorney firm:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>40 inquiries a month, 35% missed, so 14 unanswered</li>
    <li>Some of those call back on their own; most do not</li>
    <li>Recover 5 to 7 of the 14 with instant response at any hour</li>
    <li>At 30% consult-to-retain and a $3,000 average matter, that is <b>$4,500 to $6,300 a month</b>, or $54,000 to $75,600 a year</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What should never be automated</h2>
  <p>
    Here is where legal differs from every other vertical, and where a general-purpose tool becomes a professional
    responsibility problem. Four things must always be human:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>Legal advice, in any form.</b> Including the softer versions: "that sounds like a strong case," "you probably
      have until Friday," "that is usually worth about." The correct response to "do I have a case" is that only an
      attorney can answer, followed by an offer of a consult.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Accepting a client.</b> An automated intake captures information and books a consult. It does not form a
      relationship.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Conflict resolution.</b> A system can collect the data and flag a match against existing matters. Deciding what
      a potential conflict means is legal judgment.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Fees beyond a published schedule.</b> Quoting what is on your website is fine. Anything else is a conversation.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <b>The practical test:</b> ask any intake vendor to show you, in writing, the list of things their system will
      never do without a human. If they cannot produce one, they have not thought about your professional obligations,
      and those obligations are yours, not theirs.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Where to start</h2>
  <p>
    Fix leak 3 first. It is the cheapest to close and the most embarrassing to leave open. Set up an autoresponder
    today, then measure how many web inquiries you actually receive in a month. Most firms find the number is higher
    than they assumed and the response rate is worse.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then measure your after-hours call volume. If it is significant, that is leak 1, and it is the most expensive one.
  </p>

